    <h1>How to Calculate XIRR on Mutual Fund Investments</h1>
    <p class="text-muted" style="font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.7;">
      Point-to-point absolute return and CAGR work well for one-time lumpsum investments, but they completely fail for monthly Systematic Investment Plans (SIPs) where money enters the market at dozens of different NAVs.
    </p>

    <h2>What is XIRR?</h2>
    <p>
      XIRR stands for <strong>Extended Internal Rate of Return</strong>. It is the annualized discount rate that equates the Net Present Value (NPV) of all cash inflows (SIP installments, lumpsum top-ups) with the current market value (or redemption proceeds) across irregular calendar dates.
    </p>

    <h2>Calculating XIRR in Excel</h2>
    <p>
      To compute XIRR manually in Excel or Google Sheets, you need two columns:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Column A (Dates):</strong> The exact date of each cash flow (e.g., 10-Jan-2023, 10-Feb-2023).</li>
      <li><strong>Column B (Amounts):</strong> Outflows (investments) entered as <em>negative numbers</em> (e.g., -₹5,000) and the current market value as a <em>positive number</em> on today's date.</li>
      <li><strong>Formula:</strong> <code>=XIRR(B2:B38, A2:A38)</code></li>
    </ul>

